// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: c0a0c0a0c0a0 at one session and at two.
//
// Signal's PQXDH handshake, revision 2, followed by three messages of the
// Double Ratchet. The handshake follows Bhargavan, Jacomme, Kiefer and
// Schmidt, "Formal verification of the PQXDH post-quantum key agreement
// protocol for end-to-end secure messaging" (USENIX Security 2024), whose
// Figure 4 is the wire format modeled here: the paper's Alex is Alice
// below and its Blake is Bob. The ratchet follows the same two Signal
// specifications as signal.vp, the classical handshake PQXDH extends.
//
// Phase 1 then leaks EVERY Diffie-Hellman private value in the model:
// both identity keys, the signed and one-time prekeys, the X3DH
// ephemeral and all three ratchet keys. That is the paper's harvest-now-
// decrypt-later adversary (§3.2), one that records the conversation today
// and computes discrete logarithms later, say with a quantum computer.
// What it never gets is dkb, the KEM decapsulation key. Every query
// holds (c0a0c0a0c0a0): the KEM shared secret enters the initial root
// key, each ratchet step chains from the root key before it, and so
// every message key of the conversation stays out of reach — which is
// why a classical Double Ratchet seeded by PQXDH protects a whole
// conversation against this adversary, not only its first message.
// Delete ss and bss from the two session secrets and all three
// confidentiality queries fail; that is the entire reason PQXDH exists.
//
// PQXDH is X3DH with a KEM leg (paper §2.3):
//
//   1. Bob also holds a KEM key pair PQSPK_B: dkb is the decapsulation
//      (private) key and ekb the encapsulation (public) key.
//   2. His prekey bundle carries ekb and its signature under IK_B, next
//      to the signed curve prekey.
//   3. Alice encapsulates to it, (ss, ct) = KEM.encaps(ekb), and SS
//      joins the four DH values in the KDF input:
//        SK = HKDF(salt: 0, ikm: DH1 || DH2 || DH3 || DH4 || SS, info)
//   4. Her first message carries ct.
//   5. Bob decapsulates, ss = KEM.decaps(dkb, ct), and derives the same SK.
//
// SK is the Double Ratchet's initial root key exactly as in signal.vp,
// so the three messages are that model's key schedule with the KEM
// secret folded into its seed. The paper stops at the handshake — its
// models give each connection one encrypted message and no follow-ups
// (§3.1), and it verifies PQXDH in isolation (§7). Everything past
// Bob's first decryption below is therefore the Signal specifications,
// not the reference.
//
// Two of the paper's findings shape the model:
//
//   Public key confusion (§4.1.1). Revision 1 signed both prekeys under
//   one key with nothing to tell a curve key from a KEM key. Revision 2
//   requires the encodings EncodeEC and EncodeKEM to have disjoint
//   ranges (§2.1), modeled here as the public tags ec_tag and kem_tag
//   prefixed to each key before it is signed. They are written with
//   CONCAT rather than HASH because an encoding is public and
//   reversible; only the signature over it is not. Drop the tags and
//   the swap becomes available: ekb and ekbsig are replaced by gbs and
//   gbssig, Bob's own signature carries the substitution past Alice's
//   check, and she encapsulates to a curve public key. Verifpal reports
//   that swap where Bob's curve prekey is compromised while the
//   exchange runs. It does not report it on this model, and the reason
//   is the leak in phase 1: a key disclosed only downstream of the
//   check it would forge is not held in time to forge it. The deployed
//   consequence is the one the paper draws — encapsulating to a curve
//   key puts the KEM outside the assumption that makes it secure, so
//   the leg meant to survive the curve stops surviving it.
//
//   KEM re-encapsulation (§4.1.2). Under bare IND-CCA an attacker holding
//   one compromised decapsulation key can re-encapsulate ss to another
//   of Bob's KEM keys, so the two sides need not agree on which PQSPK
//   was used. Revision 2 offers a choice: put ekb in the AEAD associated
//   data, or use a KEM that binds ss to its public key (the paper's
//   SH-CR, §5.3, which it proves for Kyber). Signal chose the latter and
//   keeps AD = Encode(IK_A) || Encode(IK_B). Verifpal's KEM_DECAP inverts
//   only its own KEM_ENCAP, so it is binding by construction, and this
//   model follows the deployed choice.
//
// The paper's sharpest observation is about when bs falls, and this
// model reproduces it. Move `leaks bs` up into phase 0, so that Bob's
// signed curve prekey is compromised while the exchange runs, and the
// model answers c0a1c1a0c0a1: both of Alice's messages to Bob lose
// their authentication. The attacker computes DH1 = DH(IK_A, SPK_B)
// for itself, brings its own ephemeral and its own encapsulation to
// Bob's genuine KEM key, and hands Bob a whole first flight he accepts
// as Alice's. No signature was forged and no primitive broken — Alice
// authenticates to Bob through that one DH leg, so whoever holds SPK_B
// can be anyone to Bob. That is the ephemeral key compromise
// impersonation of Theorem 8, and it is why the leak below waits for
// phase 1. The first message stays confidential even so, the KEM leg
// being untouched; the second does not, Bob's ratchet having stepped
// onto a key the attacker chose.
//
// Modeling notes. The paper assumes every KEM key is medium-term, since
// an untrusted server can always serve the last-resort PQSPK_B and hold
// back the one-time PQOPK_B (§2.3); so dkb is `knows private`, shared
// across sessions like the signed curve prekey bs, while the one-time
// curve prekey bo is `generates` and fresh per session. One identity
// key pair both signs (XEdDSA) and does Diffie-Hellman, as deployed;
// the paper's proofs split it in two (§5.5). The one-time curve prekey
// is unsigned, as in the specification, so the attacker is free to
// substitute gbo: the queries below hold anyway, which is the model's
// version of the paper's remark that an unauthenticated OPK adds less
// than one might expect, since a server can always serve a malicious
// one or none at all (Theorem 7). The key identifiers Alice sends so
// Bob can look up his prekeys are elided, the paper finding
// them not security relevant (§5.1). So is the KDF's F prefix, the 32
// bytes of 0xFF that separate this derivation from XEdDSA's use of the
// same curve: distinct symbolic primitives separate them already, and
// the KDF input concatenation is written as a HASH of its five parts.
// As in signal.vp, the associated data models CONCAT(AD, header), both
// identity keys in a fixed order plus the header's ratchet public key,
// and each nonce is generated and sent rather than derived from the
// message key.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	// info: the KDF info string naming the application. ec_tag, kem_tag:
	// the prefixes EncodeEC and EncodeKEM put on a key before signing it.
	knows public info, ec_tag, kem_tag
	// Alice's long-term identity key pair, IK_A.
	knows private alongterm
	galongterm = PUBKEY(alongterm)
]

principal Bob[
	knows public info, ec_tag, kem_tag
	// blongterm: the identity key IK_B. bs: the signed curve prekey
	// SPK_B, which doubles as Bob's initial ratchet key pair. dkb: the
	// KEM decapsulation key PQSPK_B — the post-quantum secret everything
	// rides on.
	knows private blongterm, bs, dkb
	// bo: a one-time curve prekey OPK_B, fresh per session.
	generates bo
	gblongterm = PUBKEY(blongterm)
	gbs = PUBKEY(bs)
	gbo = PUBKEY(bo)
	// ekb: the KEM encapsulation key, the public half of dkb.
	ekb = PUBKEY(dkb)
	// Each prekey is signed under its own encoding, so a signature over a
	// curve key can never be presented as one over a KEM key.
	gbssig = SIGN(blongterm, CONCAT(ec_tag, gbs))
	ekbsig = SIGN(blongterm, CONCAT(kem_tag, ekb))
]

// Bob's prekey bundle, as fetched from the untrusted Signal server. The
// identity key is guarded — anchored out-of-band by safety numbers — while
// the two signed prekeys are protected by their signatures, not the wire.
Bob -> Alice: [gblongterm], gbs, gbssig, gbo, ekb, ekbsig

principal Alice[
	// Both signatures are checked before either prekey is used anywhere:
	// Alice aborts rather than run PQXDH against an unsigned prekey.
	_ = SIGNVERIF(gblongterm, CONCAT(ec_tag, gbs), gbssig)?
	_ = SIGNVERIF(gblongterm, CONCAT(kem_tag, ekb), ekbsig)?
	// ae1: the ephemeral EK_A. r: the KEM encapsulation randomness.
	generates ae1, r
	gae1 = PUBKEY(ae1)
	// KEM.encaps yields two values at once: the shared secret ss, kept,
	// and the ciphertext ct, sent so that Bob can recover the same ss.
	ss, ct = KEM_ENCAP(ekb, r)
	// The session secret SK: the four X3DH shared secrets and the KEM
	// secret through the KDF, under the info string naming the protocol.
	amaster = HKDF(nil, HASH(DH_KEX(gbs, alongterm), DH_KEX(gblongterm, ae1), DH_KEX(gbs, ae1), DH_KEX(gbo, ae1), ss), info)
]

principal Alice[
	generates m1, ae2
	// ae2 is Alice's initial ratchet key; Bob's half is his signed prekey.
	gae2 = PUBKEY(ae2)
	akshared1 = DH_KEX(gbs, ae2)
	// Ratchet initialization, one root-KDF step: SK is the initial root
	// key, mixed with the first ratchet DH share — KDF_RK(SK, DH(ae2, SPK_B)).
	arkab1, ackab1 = HKDF(amaster, akshared1, nil)
	// Message key derivation: KDF_CK is an HMAC keyed by the chain key,
	// and ENCRYPT expands it into the AEAD key with a zero-salt HKDF.
	akenc1 = HKDF(nil, MAC(ackab1, nil), nil)
	// The associated data binds both identities — AD = IK_A || IK_B, the
	// same order in both directions — and the header's ratchet key, so
	// replaying the ciphertext in another context fails AEAD_DEC.
	generates n_e1
	e1 = AEAD_ENC(akenc1, n_e1, m1, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gae2))
]

// Alice's initial message: her identity key, her ephemeral, the KEM
// ciphertext, and the first ratchet message.
Alice -> Bob: [galongterm], gae1, ct, gae2, n_e1, e1

principal Bob[
	// Decapsulation recovers the KEM shared secret from the ciphertext,
	// and Bob mirrors the four DH values from his side, opposite
	// private/public halves — DH_KEX(PUBKEY(a), b) = DH_KEX(PUBKEY(b), a).
	bss = KEM_DECAP(dkb, ct)
	bmaster = HKDF(nil, HASH(DH_KEX(galongterm, bs), DH_KEX(gae1, blongterm), DH_KEX(gae1, bs), DH_KEX(gae1, bo), bss), info)
]

principal Bob[
	// The receiving half of the same ratchet step.
	bkshared1 = DH_KEX(gae2, bs)
	brkab1, bckab1 = HKDF(bmaster, bkshared1, nil)
	bkenc1 = HKDF(nil, MAC(bckab1, nil), nil)
	// Checked decryption: a forged or replayed e1 halts Bob here.
	m1_d = AEAD_DEC(bkenc1, n_e1, e1, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gae2))?
]

// Message 2: Bob replies, advancing the DH ratchet with his own fresh
// ephemeral be, generated at send time (the deferred variant of §8.5).
// Note the root-key chaining: each KDF_RK takes the previous root key as
// its salt, so the KEM secret is folded into every future key.
principal Bob[
	generates m2, be
	gbe = PUBKEY(be)
	bkshared2 = DH_KEX(gae2, be)
	brkba2, bckba2 = HKDF(brkab1, bkshared2, nil)
	bkenc2 = HKDF(nil, MAC(bckba2, nil), nil)
	generates n_e2
	e2 = AEAD_ENC(bkenc2, n_e2, m2, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gbe))
]

Bob -> Alice: gbe, n_e2, e2

principal Alice[
	akshared2 = DH_KEX(gbe, ae2)
	arkba2, ackba2 = HKDF(arkab1, akshared2, nil)
	akenc2 = HKDF(nil, MAC(ackba2, nil), nil)
	m2_d = AEAD_DEC(akenc2, n_e2, e2, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gbe))?
]

// Message 3: back to Alice — another full ratchet step.
principal Alice[
	generates m3, ae3
	gae3 = PUBKEY(ae3)
	akshared3 = DH_KEX(gbe, ae3)
	arkab3, ackab3 = HKDF(arkba2, akshared3, nil)
	akenc3 = HKDF(nil, MAC(ackab3, nil), nil)
	generates n_e3
	e3 = AEAD_ENC(akenc3, n_e3, m3, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gae3))
]

Alice -> Bob: gae3, n_e3, e3

principal Bob[
	bkshared3 = DH_KEX(gae3, be)
	brkab3, bckab3 = HKDF(brkba2, bkshared3, nil)
	bkenc3 = HKDF(nil, MAC(bckab3, nil), nil)
	m3_d = AEAD_DEC(bkenc3, n_e3, e3, HASH(galongterm, gblongterm, gae3))?
]

phase[1]

// The harvest-now-decrypt-later event: every Diffie-Hellman private value
// in the conversation falls — identity keys, prekeys and ratchet keys
// alike. Conspicuously absent: dkb, the KEM decapsulation key, and r,
// the encapsulation randomness — the post-quantum side of the protocol.
principal Alice[leaks alongterm, ae1, ae2, ae3]
principal Bob[leaks blongterm, bs, bo, be]

queries[
	confidentiality? m1
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: e1
	confidentiality? m2
	authentication? Bob -> Alice: e2
	confidentiality? m3
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: e3
]
